/-
# Free subgroups generated by some of the generators (Kourovka Notebook Problem 5.29)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 5.29 (R. C. Lyndon): consider a group G given
by a presentation with m generators and n defining relations, where m ≥ n; do some m - n of the
given generators generate a free subgroup of G?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.PresentedGroup

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.PresentationFreeGeneratorsKourovka5Problem29Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 5.29 (R. C.
Lyndon). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700313 (`KOU-5.29`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

The group is `PresentedGroup (Set.range r)` for relators `r : Fin n → FreeGroup (Fin m)`; "generate
a free subgroup" is read, as intended, as freely generating it: the homomorphism from the free group
on the chosen `m - n` generators is injective.
-/

/-- Kourovka 5.29 (R. C. Lyndon): in a group `⟨x₁, …, x_m | r₁, …, r_n⟩` with `m ≥ n`, some
`m - n` of the given generators freely generate a free subgroup: the homomorphism from the free
group on them is injective. -/
def deficiencyFreeSubgroup : Prop :=
  ∀ (m n : ℕ) (r : Fin n → FreeGroup (Fin m)), n ≤ m →
    ∃ s : Finset (Fin m), s.card = m - n ∧
      Function.Injective (FreeGroup.lift fun i : s => (PresentedGroup.of (i : Fin m) :
        PresentedGroup (Set.range r)))

/--
Resolved true: the Kourovka Archive answers 5.29 "Yes, they do", citing Romanovskii, Free subgroups
of finitely presented groups (Algebra and Logic 16 (1977), 62-68). Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D.
Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue,
arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 5.29 (R. C. Lyndon); resolution: N.
S. Romanovskii, Free subgroups of finitely presented groups, Algebra and Logic 16 (1977), 62-68,
Kourovka Archive, Problem 5.29: Yes, they do (Romanovskii), https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01669436.
-/
public theorem_wanted deficiencyFreeSubgroup_holds : deficiencyFreeSubgroup

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.PresentationFreeGeneratorsKourovka5Problem29Wanted
